    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Key findings</h2>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-slate-700">
      <li><strong>Nine states tie for first.</strong> Alaska, Florida, Nevada, New Hampshire, South Dakota, Tennessee, Texas, Washington, and Wyoming levy no individual income tax — workers there keep $79,180 of a $100,000 salary.</li>
      <li><strong>Oregon takes the biggest bite.</strong> Its $8,176 state tax bill leaves just $71,004 — the lowest take-home in the country.</li>
      <li><strong>California isn\u2019t the worst.</strong> Despite its reputation, California\u2019s progressive brackets leave middle earners better off than Oregon, Hawaii, Maine, and others on $100k.</li>
      <li><strong>Local taxes aren\u2019t counted.</strong> Places with city income taxes (e.g. New York City) would rank lower if those were included.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Methodology</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700">
      Figures assume a single filer earning $100,000 in wages with no pre-tax deductions, computed with
      2026 IRS federal brackets (Revenue Procedure 2025-32), the 2026 Social Security wage base ($184,500),
      and each state\u2019s 2026 income tax brackets (Tax Foundation, as of January 1, 2026).
      State personal exemptions, credits, and local taxes are not modeled; figures are estimates.
      See our <a class="text-emerald-700 underline" href="/en-us/states/">state paycheck calculators</a> to
      run your own numbers.
    </p>
